        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>A Forest Worth Protecting</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          During the 1960s and 1970s, large areas of Thailand’s rainforest were cleared for logging and mining. The construction of Highway 401 opened much of the region to commercial exploitation, and parts of Khao Sok suffered environmental damage through forest clearance, mining and soil erosion.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Fortunately, the most remote valleys escaped the worst of this destruction. Between 1975 and 1982, student activists and communist insurgents established bases deep within the mountains. Their presence discouraged both military operations and commercial logging in these remote forests. Although an unexpected consequence of a turbulent period in Thailand’s history, many local people believe it played an important role in preserving the wilderness visitors experience today.
        </p>
        <h3 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[#163824] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-10 mb-4 pt-6 border-t border-[#12281b]/10">
          <span>A National Park is Born</span>
        </h3>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Recognising the importance of protecting what remained, Khao Sok National Park was officially established on 22 December 1980. Commercial logging and mining within the park came to an end, allowing the rainforest and its wildlife to recover naturally.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-[#223e2c]">
          Today, together with neighbouring protected areas including Khlong Phanom National Park, Khao Sok forms one of the largest remaining protected rainforest landscapes in southern Thailand.
        </p>
